WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.338
Template injection is a risk that appears when untrusted input is treated
as template code rather than ordinary data.

2
00:00:06.338 --> 00:00:14.147
At a high level, server-side template injection involves a server-side
template engine interpreting user-influenced text as template logic.

3
00:00:14.147 --> 00:00:19.333
Client-side template injection follows a similar concern in browser-side
rendering systems.

4
00:00:19.333 --> 00:00:25.862
This course avoids attack details, but the defensive lesson is clear:
template source should be trusted and controlled.

5
00:00:25.862 --> 00:00:29.447
A safe design separates template authors from application users.

6
00:00:29.447 --> 00:00:35.272
Application developers, reviewed content editors, or controlled build
processes may provide templates.

7
00:00:35.272 --> 00:00:40.138
Ordinary users, partners, imported data, and request parameters should
provide values.

8
00:00:40.138 --> 00:00:49.420
When these roles are blurred, the rendering engine may expose behavior,
data, helper functions, or environment capabilities that were never meant
for untrusted control.

9
00:00:49.420 --> 00:00:58.894
Sandboxing concepts can reduce risk in specialized systems, but they have
limitations and should not be treated as permission to execute arbitrary
untrusted template logic.

10
00:00:58.894 --> 00:01:01.967
Rendering environments should follow least privilege.

11
00:01:01.967 --> 00:01:09.777
Helpers, globals, filters, file access, network access, and sensitive
objects should be restricted to what the rendering task actually needs.

12
00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:15.410
The safest approach is still prevention: do not turn untrusted strings
into executable template logic.

13
00:01:15.410 --> 00:01:24.820
Trying to filter dangerous template syntax after the fact is fragile
because template languages are expressive and rendering behavior changes
across engines and versions.

14
00:01:24.820 --> 00:01:33.846
Defensive code should avoid user-controlled template source, avoid broad
helper exposure, and review any dynamic rendering feature as a high-risk
design decision.

15
00:01:33.846 --> 00:01:37.431
Untrusted template execution is not ordinary string formatting.

16
00:01:37.431 --> 00:01:41.080
It is a boundary that requires explicit architecture and controls.
